**Finding repaired and submitted: deviation** (was compliant).

**What changed in my assessment.** The verifier is right that Section 10 contains no IP wording at all. On reflection that is not purely a phrasing artefact: as drafted, an IP infringement claim only reaches the indemnity either (a) by arguing the broad "arising out of or in connection with the Services / Auction Platform" wording, or (b) by first establishing breach of the §9(b)(i) non-infringement warranty (p0033). Route (b) in particular gives Kubient a colourable argument that it owes no immediate duty to defend until warranty breach is proven — which defeats the point of a defence obligation. The same indirect-only problem affects the breach-of-law, data-incident and wilful-misconduct triggers.

**Redline (validated, ok:true).** A single insert after p0051 adding express prongs to §10(a):

- (vi) allegations that the Service, Auction Platform or deliverables infringe/misappropriate third-party IP or proprietary rights;
- (vii) Kubient's breach of applicable Law;
- (viii) Kubient-caused unauthorised access to or disclosure of our data or Confidential Information;
- (ix) Kubient's gross negligence or wilful misconduct.

Drafted on precedent `seed-indemn-1`. No cap is needed: §7(a) already carves indemnification out of the damages limitation, and §11 already supplies notice/control/cooperation — so we reach the preferred position with one clarifying insertion. It is consistent with warranties Kubient has already given (p0033, p0035, p0037, p0041), so it should be low-friction; fallback is the IP prong alone with customary exclusions and procure/modify/replace/refund remedies.

**Residual point the verifier raised.** I have now quoted and recorded §10(b) (p0052): AP's reciprocal indemnity does run wider than the preferred "Customer content / Customer breach of law" limit — it extends to AP's unauthorised or inappropriate use of the Services and Auction Platform, breach of any representation or warranty, and the acts or omissions of AP and its contractors. I deliberately left it unamended, since it mirrors Kubient's own prongs and reopening it would invite reciprocal narrowing of the Vendor indemnity we are strengthening. Flagged for the deal team as a judgement call rather than an oversight.
